---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('drip')!;
const b = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/drip-vs-mailchimp');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/drip-vs-mailchimp');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "What is this comparison deciding?",
    "answer": "Whether the demonstrated workflow benefit repays the change in audience, channel and operating costs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are these measured recovery results?",
    "answer": "No. The workflows are proposed acceptance exercises and the basket calculations are hypothetical gross-profit examples."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can both systems send for the same cart?",
    "answer": "Only with explicit ownership and demonstrated exclusions. During evaluation, assign each eligible cart to one recovery owner per channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must survive migration?",
    "answer": "Permission provenance, opt-outs, pending-cart treatment and a ledger of messages already sent must survive the change."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Compare a working newsletter audience with an event-driven recovery rebuild.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate Drip", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Prove the required cart workflow and account access."}, {"label": "Evaluate Mailchimp", "tool": "Mailchimp", "reason": "Compare the validated operating benefit with staying in the current setup."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Start with the audience you actually operate</h2>
<p>A store with a working Mailchimp newsletter should first ask whether its Shopify recovery flow meets the brief. Moving to Drip is justified when a demonstrated workflow requirement repays rebuilding audiences, templates and reporting. Run the same cart through both before comparing interface preferences. A larger imported audience is not automatically a larger reachable recovery audience.</p>
<h2>Read the first step and follow-up eligibility separately</h2>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/create-abandoned-cart-customer-journey/">Mailchimp’s abandoned-cart flow guide</a> distinguishes its cart-specific message from additional Send email or Send SMS steps: those extra steps send only to subscribed contacts. Therefore, a first reminder delivered to a test contact does not establish eligibility for the rest of a series. Confirm the permitted audience and actual account behavior before enabling messages. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Test the event boundary in Drip</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/42835644176397-Shopify-Updated-a-Cart-vs-Checkout-Updated-Created-Events">Drip’s Shopify event guide</a> separates identified add-to-cart activity from checkout-created/updated events. It also warns that products added after an email is queued will not appear in that email. The practical comparison is the rendered product, variant, currency and return link, not a generic label such as advanced automation.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance exercise: newsletter reader to purchaser</h2>
<p>Use a subscribed newsletter reader, a non-subscribed checkout contact and an unsubscribed former buyer. Record their source status, the event received, the first message decision and each follow-up decision. Add a product before checkout, then change its size while the reminder waits. Compare the actual queued email with the current checkout. No exercise here is a measured product result.</p>
<p>For the repeat buyer, purchase during the wait and confirm that the remaining reminders stop. Replay the event and start a second cart to check re-entry. Keep the cart key distinct from the email address so a historical purchase does not accidentally suppress all future carts, and an old event does not restart a completed one.</p>
<h2>Use merchandising answers before a coupon</h2>
<p>For mobile distraction, send a concise return link rather than assuming a price objection. For an unavailable size, remove the invalid purchase prompt and offer an available alternative only if accurate. A gift-message problem or subscription toggle calls for instructions; an international shipping objection calls for the actual delivery terms. Financing and BNPL questions need a clear route back to supported payment information, while bundle or MOQ failures need corrected cart rules. These are separate content branches to prove in each editor, not reasons to prescribe one universal timing ladder.</p>
<h2>Model the incentive cost on two different baskets</h2>
<p>Hypothetical fixed-goods-cost examples: a $195 basket at 35% gross margin has $126.75 goods cost and $68.25 gross profit. An 8% code reduces revenue to $179.40 and gross profit to $52.65, a $15.60 reduction. A $595 basket at 59% margin has $243.95 goods cost and $351.05 gross profit; an illustrative 18% code leaves $487.90 revenue and $243.95 gross profit, a $107.10 reduction. These figures exclude shipping, processing, returns, tax and software. Neither percentage is an optimal cap.</p>
<h2>Price a migration against staying</h2>
<p>Request quotes using the same reachable profiles, expected sends, required flow steps and support scope. Add the hours to recreate templates, validate segments and reconcile reports. Compare that migration cost with the cost of repairing the current Mailchimp flow. A cheaper headline subscription can lose its advantage if the store must maintain duplicate audiences or rebuild its campaign calendar.</p>
<p>For an evaluation, keep incentive policy constant and allocate comparable eligible customers to one recovery owner each. Deduplicate orders against Shopify and compare gross profit after variable costs, opt-outs and operator hours. Use a predeclared observation period that includes returns; small samples support a cautious decision, not a claimed lift percentage.</p>
<h2>Cut over without reactivating old suppressions</h2>
<p>Export permission provenance, unsubscribes, bounces, segment rules and pending-cart state before changing senders. Reconcile the destination counts by status and inspect sample histories, rather than converting every imported customer into a subscriber. Choose whether existing waiting carts finish in the old flow or stop there; new carts alone should enter the replacement during the transition.</p>
<p>Pause conflicting Shopify and app recovery sends, keep newsletter ownership explicit, and switch a limited eligible cohort after the acceptance exercise passes. For rollback, stop the new queue before restoring the previous sender and retain a ledger of carts already contacted. Replaying the entire historical event stream would defeat that boundary. Stay on Mailchimp if the validated flow is sufficient; choose Drip when the specific workflow benefit exceeds this operating cost.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
